# Datsun 120Y four-door saloon (B210), 1973-1977.
#
# Sold in Britain as the Datsun 120Y from the autumn of 1973; Datsun UK's
# April 1974 chart heads the range "120Y Sunny Range". The four-door saloon is
# modelled; the two-door saloon, coupe, estate and van are not represented.
# https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nissan_Sunny
#
# Dimensions 3.950 x 1.545 x 1.360 m, wheelbase 2.340 m (Wikipedia infobox;
# the longer figures in the same box are the wagon, the Excellent and the
# US-bumper cars).
#
# Shape reference: image 1 is about 20 degrees off the side axis, so the flank
# is estimated within the published dimensions; images 2-4 include a UK
# registered car, VJA 355S, carrying its Datsun 120Y boot badge.
#
# Colours: grade (a) for the codes and (d) for the names. Datsun UK's April
# 1974 chart lists codes 216, 225, 226, 227, 931 and 932 for the two- and
# four-door saloons, without names. White, Red, Blue and Yellow are plain names
# chosen to suit the period. Weights 12, 10, 9, 8, total 39, provisional.
#
# Speed 145 km/h. Comfort 40/390, on the line 330 + 1.5 x 40. Classes 0,1,2.
# Dates: intro October 1973, the autumn launch; retire staggered 1988-1992 as a
# road tail.
#
# Metres master: citycars/4.5/nissan-sunny-b210.blend. Interchange:
# citycars/4.5/nissan-sunny-b210.dae. References:
# citycars/references/nissan-sunny-b210/ (gitignored; Wikimedia Commons).
#
# Accepted deviations: the flank is a reasoned estimate from the most oblique
# of the four profile references, bounded by the published dimensions and
# wheelbase. A single-colour roof is modelled, the vinyl roof of the
# photographed car is not copied, the accessory spotlights are omitted and the
# badge lettering is simplified.


obj=citycar
name=Datsun120Y(White)
copyright=JamesPetts

DistributionWeight=12
intro_year=1973
intro_month=10
retire_year=1992
retire_month=6
speed=145
length=3
class=0,1,2
maximum_comfortable_journey_minutes=40
maximum_tolerable_journey_minutes=390

